.TH "cbor::schema::encode" "3" "2026-11-01" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH NAME
cbor::schema::encode \- writes a struct as one Packed CBOR message
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<cbor/cbor.hpp>\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
static std::expected<std::string, cbor::error> encode(T const &value)
    \f[B]requires\f[R](has_describe_members<std::remove_cv_t<T>> && tags_registered<T>());

\f[B]template\f[R] <\f[B]class\f[R] Target>
    \f[B]requires\f[R](has_describe_members<std::remove_cv_t<T>> && tags_registered<T>())
static std::expected<std::size_t, cbor::error> encode(T const &value, Target &&target);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Member of cbor::schema<T>.
.PP
Writes \f[CR]value\f[R] as one Packed CBOR message (wire).
The first form returns a new \f[CR]std::string\f[R].
The second form appends to \f[CR]target\f[R] and returns the count of
bytes of the message.
\f[CR]target\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::string\f[R], a container of
\f[CR]char\f[R], a \f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R] or a message writer.
Only public members are written.
.PP
Each field has a head of fixed width.
So a value does not change the offset of a field.
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]no_buffer_space\f[R]: a \f[CR]std::span<char>\f[R] is shorter than
the message.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]value_too_large\f[R]: the message is longer than 2\(ha32 \- 1
bytes.
.IP \(bu 2
An error of the message writer comes back as its \f[CR]cbor::error\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
A struct with no tag, two structs with one tag, or a tag of a standard
type does not compile.
.SH NOTES
O(i + n), i the count of items of the value, n the size of the message.
The first form makes one \f[CR]std::string\f[R].
A container grows once.
A span allocates nothing.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const bytes = cbor::schema<tire>::encode(tire{17, 2.5f});
std::string out = \(dqx\(dq;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const n = cbor::schema<tire>::encode(tire{17, 2.5f}, out);
use(out.size() == 1 + *n);   \f[I]// true\f[R]
.EE
